# =============================================================================
# .gitattributes
#
# Line endings are a real hazard for this repository, and one that cost time.
#
# setup.ps1 was authored with Unix (LF) line endings. Windows PowerShell 5.1
# mis-parses a .ps1 with LF endings in some constructs - it fell out of string
# context mid-file and tried to execute the prose inside a comment block. The
# script had passed a syntax check under PowerShell 7 on Linux, where LF is
# fine, so the check confirmed nothing about the environment it would run in.
#
# * text=auto lets Git normalise text files to LF in the repository and check
# them out with the platform convention. The explicit .ps1 rule forces CRLF on
# checkout regardless of platform, because that is what Windows PowerShell
# expects and a stranger cloning this will be on Windows.
#
# The script itself no longer depends on line endings - the here-strings that
# broke were replaced with string arrays. This file is the second line of
# defence, not the fix.
# =============================================================================

* text=auto

*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

*.py   text eol=lf
*.sh   text eol=lf

# Binary: never normalise, never diff as text.
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pbix binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.pdf  binary
